  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Why Houston Is a Great Vintage City</h2>
<p>Houston is huge, sprawling and constantly rebuilding, and that churn is good news for anyone who loves old things. Estates turn over, bungalows get renovated, mid-century ranch houses in the suburbs get cleared out, and a lot of that material ends up in shops, resale stores and markets around town. The catch is that it is spread out. There is no one street where you can see it all, so the smartest approach is to think in terms of areas and plan a loop.</p>
<p>Below is a general, evergreen look at what each part of the inner city tends to be good for. Individual shops open, close and move all the time, so treat this as a map of tendencies rather than a directory, and always check hours before you go.</p>

<h2>The Heights</h2>
<p>The Heights is where many Houstonians start, and for good reason. The area around 19th Street in the Heights has long had a walkable, small-town main street feel, with boutiques, gift shops and resale spots close together. Beyond that strip, the neighborhood is dotted with antique and vintage shops tucked into old storefronts and converted houses.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best for:</strong> vintage clothing, jewelry, small home goods, gifts, and the occasional great lamp or side table.</li>
<li><strong>Style:</strong> park once and walk. Weekend afternoons get busy, so mornings are calmer.</li>
<li><strong>Pair it with:</strong> a coffee stop and a stroll on the hike-and-bike trail that runs through the neighborhood.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Montrose</h2>
<p>Montrose has always had an artsy, eclectic streak, and its resale scene reflects that. Expect a mix of curated vintage clothing, consignment, records, odd art and the kind of shop where the owner has strong opinions and great taste.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best for:</strong> statement clothing, band tees, vinyl, prints and quirky decor.</li>
<li><strong>Style:</strong> shops are spread along a few main roads, so plan short drives between clusters.</li>
<li><strong>Pair it with:</strong> the Museum District next door if you want a culture break.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Rice Village</h2>
<p>Rice Village is better known for boutiques and restaurants than for dusty antique malls, but it holds a special place in local market history. The team behind Vintique Flea created Rice Village Flea before launching Vintique, so the idea of a well-curated, walkable market with good food has deep roots here.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best for:</strong> polished resale fashion, gifts and a pleasant afternoon of browsing.</li>
<li><strong>Style:</strong> compact and walkable; parking can be tight on weekends.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Washington Avenue and the Arts District</h2>
<p>The Washington Avenue corridor runs from near downtown out toward Memorial Park, and the stretch around the <a href="/guides/washington-avenue-arts-district/">Washington Avenue Arts District</a> is home to working artist studios, galleries and warehouse spaces. This is where Vintique Flea has always set up, inside Silver Street Studios at 2000 Edwards Street.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best for:</strong> markets, maker goods, art, and big-ticket finds like furniture and salvage when a market is on.</li>
<li><strong>Style:</strong> destination shopping. Come for an event, then explore studios nearby.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Farther Out: Suburbs and Highway Antique Malls</h2>
<p>Some of the best furniture deals in the region are outside the Loop. Multi-dealer antique malls along the major highways and in older suburbs often have more floor space, which means more dining sets, dressers and <a href="/shop/mid-century-modern/">mid-century modern</a> case goods. Prices can be friendlier, but you trade walkability for drive time.</p>
<div class="tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> If you are hunting for something big, bring measurements of your room and doorways, plus a truck or a friend with one. Many dealers will hold a piece briefly, but not forever.</div>

<h2>Thrift Versus Vintage Versus Antique</h2>
<p>It helps to know what kind of store you are walking into:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Thrift stores</strong> sell donated goods at low prices. You do the sorting, and the thrill is in the hunt.</li>
<li><strong>Vintage shops</strong> are curated. Someone has already found the good stuff, cleaned it and priced it for its era and condition.</li>
<li><strong>Antique malls</strong> house many dealers under one roof, with a wide range of quality and price.</li>
<li><strong>Flea markets</strong> bring dozens of independent sellers together for a limited time, often with food and drinks. You get variety and the chance to talk directly with the person who found the piece.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Where Vintique Flea Fits In</h2>
<p>Vintique Flea is a curated indoor marketplace, which solves two classic Houston problems at once: the heat and the driving. Instead of hopping between ten shops, you get mid-century furniture, <a href="/shop/vintage-clothing/">vintage clothing</a>, architectural salvage, rustic decor, antiques and collectibles in one air-conditioned space, plus local food trucks and beer and wine. Past editions have run over a Saturday and Sunday, and kids 16 and under have been admitted free, which makes it an easy family outing.</p>
<p>Keep an eye on the <a href="/events/">events page</a> for the next edition, browse the <a href="/vendors/">vendors</a> to see who might be there, and read our <a href="/guides/how-to-shop-a-flea-market/">flea market tips</a> before you go.</p>

<h2>Building Your Own Vintage Loop</h2>
<p>A good Houston vintage day usually looks like this: one walkable neighborhood in the morning, lunch, then a market or a big antique mall in the afternoon when you have the energy to haul something home. Group stops by area, keep a list of what you are actually looking for, and leave room for the thing you did not know you needed. That last one is usually the best purchase of the day.</p>
